@see or3/teach-the-process-not-the-facility, or3/authorship-for-first-time-contributors

! conclusion: Scenarios From Lived Experience

  + context: You are building teaching scenarios about people whose situation you have not lived.

  + IF:
    Your programme teaches students to work with people in difficult circumstances — illness, distress, discrimination, dying — and you need practice material.

  + HOWEVER:
    Written vignettes and mannequins are emotionally sterile. Students perform the procedure correctly and learn nothing about the encounter, and scenarios authored entirely inside the department tend to reproduce the department's assumptions about the people it describes.

  + THEN:
    Co-design the scenarios with trained actors who bring relevant lived experience, and with service-user groups and community partners from the populations concerned. Bring in clinical or subject experts for the specifics. Design for emotional safety deliberately — the realism that makes the learning work is the same realism that can harm participants and students, so the debrief and the limits are part of the scenario, not an afterthought.

  + BECAUSE:
    Authenticity is what produces the emotional openness that empathy is learned through, and it cannot be written from outside. Involving the represented community also corrects representation errors before they are taught to a cohort of future practitioners.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Identify one scenario in your programme where the represented group was not involved in writing it, and start there.]
    next[Plan for empathy fatigue in students and for the emotional load on actors, explicitly, in the session design.]
